::CW_U06_L04_GP_Q01::Poem problem: The line says, "I felt sad." Feedback asks for a stronger image. Which revision best fits?{
~A. I felt very sad and sadder.#Incorrect. This repeats the feeling.
~B. Sadness is a feeling.#Incorrect. This defines rather than revises.
=C. Rain gathered on the sill until the glass forgot the street.#Correct. This uses a concrete image to suggest sadness.
~D. I deleted the poem.#Incorrect. The poem can be revised.
}

::CW_U06_L04_GP_Q02::Poem problem: The final choice happens too quickly. Which revision best uses line breaks?{
=A. Place the final phrase on its own line so the reader pauses before the decision.#Correct. This uses line breaks to control pacing.
~B. Add random breaks after every letter.#Incorrect. Random breaks distract readers.
~C. Remove all line breaks forever.#Incorrect. Line breaks can support meaning.
~D. Change the poem into a grocery list.#Incorrect. This does not address pacing.
}

::CW_U06_L04_GP_Q03::Which revision best improves style through precise word choice?{
~A. Replace every short word with the longest word possible.#Incorrect. Longer words are not automatically more precise.
~B. Add vague words like nice and good everywhere.#Incorrect. Vague words weaken style.
~C. Ignore the intended effect.#Incorrect. Style should support effect.
=D. Replace "walked slowly" with "shuffled" if the speaker is tired and hesitant.#Correct. The verb is more precise and supports character effect.
}

::CW_U06_L04_GP_Q04::Which explanation best connects a style revision to reader effect?{
~A. I changed words because I had to.#Incorrect. This does not explain effect.
=B. I moved the final image to its own line so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This explains the craft choice and reader effect.
~C. I made random changes.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful.
~D. I used longer words to sound smarter.#Incorrect. Style is about precision, not showing off.
}

::CW_U06_L04_GP_Q05::Which is a common poetry revision mistake?{
~A. Replacing a vague feeling with a concrete image.#Incorrect. This is usually a strong revision.
~B. Explaining how a line break affects pacing.#Incorrect. This is useful craft reasoning.
=C. Adding random line breaks because poems should look unusual.#Correct. Line breaks should support meaning, emphasis, sound, or pacing.
~D. Revising repetition when it becomes distracting.#Incorrect. That is a targeted revision.
}
